This page's source MathML wraps every unit-vector/position-vector symbol
(r, i, j, k, s, u, a, v in the vector-valued-function exercises) in
<mstyle mathvariant="bold" mathsize="normal"><mtext>...</mtext></mstyle>,
but extract-exercises.py's mstyle handling doesn't look at mathvariant, so
the converter emitted plain \text{r}/\text{i}/\text{j}/\text{k}/etc. instead
of \mathbf{r}/\mathbf{i}/\mathbf{j}/\mathbf{k}/etc., silently dropping the
boldface that distinguishes a vector from a scalar throughout the whole
page. Rewritten to \mathbf{...} here; same systematic converter gap already
noted for section 3.1's review, patched per-file rather than fixing the
converter.

Two one-character exceptions where a vector's derivative appeared as plain
(non-bold, not even \text-wrapped) italic r/u alongside its bold counterpart
in the same sentence -- $\|r'(t)\|$ next to $\mathbf{r}(t)$ in the speed
definition, and $u'(t)$ next to $\mathbf{u}(t)$ in a cross-product
derivative -- were corrected to \mathbf{r}'(t) and \mathbf{u}'(t) for
consistency with every other occurrence of the same vector-valued function.
Likewise the bare, unwrapped "T(t)" in "Find the unit tangent vector T(t)"
was wrapped as $\mathbf{T}(t)$, matching the vector notation used
throughout.

Also: split two exercises that fused separate equations and connecting
English ("for", "and") into a single math span with manual \, spacing into
separate $...$ spans with the conjunction in prose, per the review
checklist's "keep English outside math mode" rule; wrapped several bare
prose variables ("value of t", "radius b", "as t approaches", "x-axis",
"y-axis") in math mode; added parentheses to \ln t^{3} for clarity; removed
a stray space before a period after $2\pi$; and removed a few instances of
spurious \, immediately after an opening bracket/pipe that had no visual
effect.

A later pass found three tangent-vector exercises where the evaluation point
followed the vector-valued function via a semicolon fused inside one math
span (e.g. "...\mathbf{k};t=\frac{\pi}{3}$"), unlike a fourth, already-correct
item in the same list that joins the function and point as two separate
$...$ spans with "; " between them ("...\mathbf{k}$; $t=\ln(2)$" -- that
two-span joiner is a different, unaffected construct). Split each of the
three into two comma-joined spans, e.g. "...\mathbf{k}$, $t=\frac{\pi}{3}$",
to match this file's established single-span-parameter convention.

